## Quick start

```bash
make quickstart      # first run: backend/.env from the example, development stack, migrations{% if use_jwt %}, admin user{% endif %}
make dev             # the development stack, every day after the first run (reload on change)
make prod-local      # the production stack on this machine: production settings, local secrets
make help            # every command, by area
```

Then:

- API: <http://localhost:{{ backend_port }}>, reference at <http://localhost:{{ backend_port }}/docs>
{%- if use_jwt %}
- Development administrator: `admin@example.com` / `admin123` (created by `make seed`, for development only)
{%- endif %}
{%- if use_frontend %}
- Frontend: `make dev-frontend` serves it on <http://localhost:{{ frontend_port }}>, or `cd frontend && bun install && bun dev`
{%- endif %}

`make dev` is idempotent: it builds the backend image, starts the stack with the source
mounted and reload on change, waits for PostgreSQL and applies pending migrations. Re-run it
after any change.

To run the backend on the host instead (breakpoints, IDE debugging):

```bash
make install       # backend dependencies, API contract{% if enable_precommit %}, pre-commit hooks{% endif %}
make docker-db{% if enable_redis %} docker-redis{% endif %}
make db-upgrade
make run
```

